# The annotations of $AC38-$BF0C in its plain view: the decryption chain and the SpeedLock 7 loader, the source of
# src/loader/chain.asm and src/loader/chain/*.asm (../../kit/mkgame.py --part src/loader/chain.json). Format:
# ../../../../docs/ann-format.md. The plain view is each byte after the last layer that rewrites it
# (tools/speedlock.py plain); tools/speedlock.py master turns it back into the tape's bytes, identical.
# Witnesses: the chain run of tools/speedlock.py (SkoolKit's simulator, from the tape's BASIC: 103 layers and the
# loader, 2.87 M instructions); c1 (work/stage1/rec/c1) for the loader: L1 of $B8F9-$BF0C, frames 2288-23974.

@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the attributes
@equ $8000 AttrBuffer 768 ; the loading screen's attributes as block 15 loads them, copied to the screen in steps
@equ $9000 TimeBuffer 255 ; the edge counts TimeCheck measures
@equ $BF0D Stage2 ; the loader's second stage (src/loader/stage2.asm)
@equ $BF30 BlockTable ; the block table, five bytes a row (src/loader/stage2.asm)

@module chain ; The decryption chain: 103 layers, each rewriting the rest and running into it
@about Stage1 (src/tape/basic.asm) returns into $AC38. Each layer first shifts the 30 bytes behind its own LDDR down by one, which garbles the code already run, then rewrites every byte from its own end up to $BF0C and falls into the bytes it has just rewritten: the next layer. Three kinds of rewrite take turns: p = key + c with a CPL or a subtraction after (68 layers), p = key - c with an XOR after (12), and p = c - 1 (22), where c is the byte before and p after. The keys come from the R register, which Layer000 sets to 12, and from constants and the loop counters, never from the bytes, so each byte's transform is fixed by its address.
@about tools/speedlock.py runs the chain in SkoolKit's simulator both ways: from the tape it gives this plain view, from this plain view it gives the tape's bytes back (identical, tools/build.sh). The last layer falls into LoaderStart $B8F9.

$AC38 c Layer000 ; Layer 0: rewrite the 4785 bytes $AC5C-$BF0C (ADD A,(HL)), run into them
> Shifts the 30 bytes up to its own LDDR down by one (the code already run is garbled), then rewrites each byte c of $AC5C-$BF0C into p = NOT ((R XOR D) + c), D = $F3 (R the refresh register as LD A,R reads it). Known from the code and the chain run of tools/speedlock.py (all 104 layers, the tape masters back identical).
$AC50 Layer000Loop ; one byte a pass

